mode script

// #269/#326 regression: the "*.method" wildcard must not INVENT
// reachability. Tuple.delete dispatches teardown by runtime name --
// `dyn_call(tn, "delete", 2, ...)` over the element type it stored -- and
// the scavenger answers that with a wildcard, because no static call site
// names the target. The wildcard used to expand over every top-level
// declaration in the program, core included, so a single `..Dynamic` call
// (which lowers to a Tuple) kept `delete` and `clone` on types nothing
// mentions: core/tuple.til imports ast, so a 30-line user program was
// emitting Expr_delete, StructDef_delete, NodeType_delete and the rest of
// the compiler's own data model -- 2305 lines of C, 105 functions, for
// three structs and a println.
//
// A by-name dispatch can only land on a type the program mentions somehow:
// it needs a value of that type (layout and constructor are ordinary edges)
// or its name (the dyn_type_to_str(T) Ident, or a Str literal, which
// collect_refs roots when it spells a type). So the wildcard reaches the
// types the walk already reached, and nothing else.
//
// mode script so the reachability walk is the ordinary one: `mode test`
// roots conservatively and would keep both types regardless.

// Reached: constructed and read below.
WcKept := struct {
    wk_n := 0
}

// Not reached: declared, never constructed, never named as a string. The
// wildcard used to root WcUnused.delete, and that root kept the struct.
WcUnused := struct {
    wu_text := ""
}

wc_count := func(vals: ..Dynamic) returns USize {
    return vals.len()
}

k := WcKept(wk_n = 7)
println(k.wk_n.to_str())
println(wc_count(1, "two", true).to_str())
